        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "8e80bf87ac4aa7da7a198a7214d94fcbeb0b196a",
            "parents": [
                "8290ca426d12519eb182032837a5eb3baf05567e"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/70/85070/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "PeterYinGit",
                "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                "username": "PeterYinGit"
            },
            "createdOn": 1762753826,
            "author": {
                "name": "PeterYinGit",
                "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                "username": "PeterYinGit"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 823,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "change is incompatible with https://dbus.freedesktop.org/doc/dbus-api-design.html\n\nCan we define it as an array, perhaps `in_Offsets` ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 823,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Shouldn't this be handled as a separate refactoring?  Considering `inN` is already supported by a bunch of other properties here, I don't think it is fair to single out this change that is just continuing the same existing pattern."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 823,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "### separate refactorings were unsuccessful\n\n> Shouldn't this be handled as a separate refactoring? \n\nIf that were possible, i would be in favor of doing that separately.\n\nUnfortunately the schemas are stable and apparently it's not acceptable to remove properties at a later point even if all upstream configs and reactors are migrated.\n\nRefer to the feedback i got on\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/84024\n\nwhere i tried to do such a refactoring.\n\n### the technical debt\n\n> I don't think it is fair to single out this change that is just continuing the same existing pattern.\n\nI'm fine with adding technical debt as long as we already know that it's okay to refactor that later / separately. Currently that's not the case.\n\n### the existing pattern\n\n> I don't think it is fair to single out this change that is just continuing the same existing pattern.\n\n`patternProperties` is only used as part of 2 schemas currently, both of which are part of `legacy.json`. \n\nAnd it's an existing pattern only within the `PSUSensor` schema. The overall pattern is to not use `patternProperties` which is what most of the schemas are doing.\n\n### many simple options to move forward\n\nI proposed a simple way to solve this issue with a concrete suggestion.\nI was not looking for any large or unreasonable amount of work."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 823,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> change is incompatible with https://dbus.freedesktop.org/doc/dbus-api-design.html\n\nYou can't throw a spec link like this and use it as justification to block a patch making a trivial extension to something that exists.  Yes, the DBus design of EM sucks, and is hard to use with PDI.  This change existing doesn't make it any harder to use, and is in line with the APIs that currently exist.  Refactorings being unsuccessful aren't relevant on whether we should accept this patch."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 823,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> Can we define it as an array, perhaps in_Offsets ?\n\nThis would mean that \"in\" type sensors have a completely different Dbus API and backend parser implementation.  That's not something I'm willing to enforce on an author when this patch is trivial and consistent both on the implementation side and the schema side."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 823,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> You can't throw a spec link like this and use it as justification to block a patch making a trivial extension to something that exists.\n\nWhy not? Creating stable external interfaces to EM and the BMC as a whole is not a 'trivial' thing and great care should be taken to do the right thing here.\n\nLet's not pretend we are making a small change to something which can easily be cleaned up at a later point.\n\n> This would mean that \"in\" type sensors have a completely different Dbus API and backend parser implementation.\n\nIs it a heavy lift? no.\n\n> when this patch is trivial and consistent both on the implementation side and the schema side.\n\nactually the PSUSensor schema is inconsistent with most of the other schemas that exist in EM.\n\nYou are choosing the scope and radius of consistency to suit your argument.\n\n- within this specific (legacy) schema, the change is consistent\n- within EM schemas, this change is inconsistent (using patternProperties)\n- in context of DBus usage, the change is inconsistent with spec"
                }
            ],
